#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096618 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096418} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f15a60dd-f6a7-577d-bdbc-148032de94c8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050690}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186676062} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233809} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186586552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186586560} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657612} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657613} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657623} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657627}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing an Exponential or Logarithmic Function"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115378 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114957} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74b356ed-a543-598f-b7d6-ce04cffd3675" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049370}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115782} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363813}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Falling Body Model With Linear Drag"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115379 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114960} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "474ea374-b644-5444-9b04-f8d76187176f" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115785} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363833} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363836} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363881}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Terminal Velocity"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115783 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "265836f7-7db8-5001-8308-e671ee3e814c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113665} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f18bb050-18cc-5928-9d8c-896a3ef3632b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363762}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363762} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecd082fa-e103-5217-aeaa-809d0e229d80" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "113b60a6-133a-57f7-b5c5-f7c669afe5dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-334554" :question/problem "A supply crate of mass $18\\,\\text{kg}$ is dropped vertically downward with an initial velocity of $2.4\\,\\text{m/s}$ and instantaneous acceleration of $8.2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Let $v(t)$ be the crate's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction. The air resistance on the crate is proportional to its velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\n\nFind, rounded to one decimal place, the velocity of the supply crate after $4$ seconds.\nAssume that only gravity, $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and drag act on the crate.\n\nThe velocity of the supply crate after $4$ seconds is\n{{field-1}}$\\,\\text{m/s}.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, note that only two forces act on the crate: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw=mg=18\\cdot 9.8=176.4\\,\\text{N},\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity (and negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n\n$$\nD=-kv,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Hence, the resultant force acting on the crate is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=176.4-kv.\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\ 176.4-kv & =18\\,\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{k}{18}v & =9.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that, at a particular moment, the crate’s velocity is $2.4\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $8.2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Substituting these values into the ODE, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n8.2+\\frac{k}{18}\\cdot 2.4=9.8\\,⟹\\,k=12\\,\\text{kg/s}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{2}{3}v=9.8\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${v}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{v}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{2}{3}{v}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{v}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-2t/3},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{v}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${v}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{v}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{2}{3}\\alpha =9.8\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =14.7\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${v}_{p}(t)=14.7.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nv(t)={v}_{c}(t)+{v}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-2t/3}+14.7.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the crate is released with an initial velocity of $2.4\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $v(0)=2.4,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =A{e}^{-2(0)/3}+14.7 \\\\ 2.4 & =A+14.7 \\\\ A & =-12.3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $v(t):$\n\n$$\nv(t)=-12.3{e}^{-2t/3}+14.7\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of the supply crate after $t=4$ seconds is\n\n$$\nv(4)=-12.3{e}^{-2(4)/3}+14.7=13.845…≈13.8\\,\\text{m/s},\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115785 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "45e65d4c-3298-5398-82d7-b45cfbd26c0f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113667} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df8663d4-6cd9-51c3-8f9c-05a3eec71857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363764}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363764} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1d2a0e2-c9a1-5f21-95bd-334eccb632ba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cfb581b3-3b37-5c68-b506-d7706bce3e3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-334575" :question/problem "A ball of mass $16\\,\\text{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v$ be its velocity, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the ball is proportional to its velocity and acts in the opposite direction to the motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the ball’s velocity is $16\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $4.9\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nWhat is the terminal velocity ${v}_{\\text{term}}$ of the body? Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the body are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\n${v}_{\\text{term}}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/s}$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Terminal velocity is the constant speed a falling object approaches as the upward resistive force (drag) grows to exactly balance the downward gravitational force.\nThat is, at terminal velocity (a constant), acceleration is zero, so by $F=ma,$ the resultant force is zero.\n\nFirst, note that only two forces act on the ball: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw=mg=16\\cdot 9.8=156.8\\,\\text{N},\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity (and negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n\n$$\nD=-kv,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Hence, the resultant force acting on the ball is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=156.8-kv.\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\ 156.8-kv & =16a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that, at a particular moment, the ball’s velocity is $16\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $4.9\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}156.8-16k & =16\\cdot 4.9 \\\\ 156.8-16k & =78.4 \\\\ -16k & =-78.4 \\\\ k & =4.9\\,\\text{kg/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the resultant force acting on the ball is\n\n$$\nF=156.8-4.9v.\n$$\n\nWhen the ball reaches terminal velocity ${v}_{\\text{term}},$ the resultant force is zero:\n\n$$\n156.8-4.9{v}_{\\text{term}}=0\\,⟹\\,{v}_{\\text{term}}=\\frac{156.8}{4.9}=32\n$$\n\nTherefore, the terminal velocity of the ball is ${v}_{\\text{term}}=32\\,\\text{m/s}.$"}] [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id 